            Capacity is a wash; the two batteries differ by two watt-hours. The meaningful gap is the inverter. EcoFlow rates the River 3 Plus at 600 W, which covers a small blender, a projector, a mini fridge, a soldering station or a CPAP with room to spare. Jackery lists the Explorer 300 Plus at 300 W, which is plenty for laptops, a camera charger, string lights, a fan and a 12 V cooler, but not for anything with a heating element or a meaningful motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The River also has an expansion path: EcoFlow's optional extra battery for the River 3 series roughly triples its capacity, turning a day-trip box into a weekend box without buying a second station. The Jackery stays at 288 Wh for its whole life. For a unit that will mostly charge devices that is no hardship, but it does mean the only upgrade is a second purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Worked Example: Two Nights at a Campsite</h3>

          <p>
            A 12 V compressor cooler averaging 45 W for 48 hours uses about 2,160 Wh, which is already far beyond either unit on its own and surprises a lot of first-time buyers. Drop the cooler to ice and the budget looks very different: LED lights at 10 W for 10 hours (100 Wh), four phone charges (80 Wh) and a camera battery or two (30 Wh) total about 210 Wh, comfortably inside either unit's roughly 245 Wh of usable energy, even before a small panel tops it up. For a trip that includes a powered cooler, either add 100 to 200 W of solar or step up to the 1 kWh class. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a> works through full trip budgets like this one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small-class winner: EcoFlow, on the strength of double the output and the expansion option. If your list is strictly phones, laptops and lights, the Jackery is just as capable, and for that job you may not need a power station at all; our roundup of <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> covers lighter, cheaper ways to keep devices alive.
          </p>
