          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the 300 W Gap Means</h3>

          <p>
            A 1,500 W ceiling and an 1,800 W ceiling sound close, but a lot of household appliances live between them. Many full-size microwaves draw 1,200 to 1,500 W from the wall, kettles and toasters often draw 1,500 to 1,800 W, and a hair dryer on high is commonly around 1,800 W. On the Jackery, those loads either trip the inverter or leave no room for the fridge to cycle on at the same moment. On the EcoFlow, most of them fit, as long as nothing else large is running. If your outage plan includes making coffee or heating food in a microwave, that is a practical reason to lean EcoFlow. For motor loads the comparison is closer: a refrigerator compressor may pull 600 to 1,200 W for a moment at start-up, and the Jackery's 3,000 W surge rating swallows that comfortably.
