          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Worked Example: A Two-Day Storm Outage</h3>

          <p>
            A household wants to keep a fridge (1.5 kWh/day), a chest freezer (about 1 kWh/day), the furnace blower (about 3 kWh/day in cold weather), lights and internet (about 0.8 kWh/day) and a well pump that uses perhaps 1 kWh/day. That is roughly 7.3 kWh a day. The Delta Pro 3 on its own provides about 3,480 Wh usable, just under half a day at that rate. One extra battery roughly doubles it, and 1,600 W of panels in a sunny spell can put back several kilowatt-hours a day at a realistic 60 to 75% of rating over four to five peak sun hours. That is how battery systems reach multi-day coverage: storage to get through the night, solar to refill during the day, and some discipline about what stays on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands can be configured to do this job. EcoFlow's advantage is that the Delta Pro 3 platform has been through more generations of home-backup hardware and offers more ways to integrate with a house. Before you commit to either, compare the full installed cost of station, extra batteries, panel hardware and electrician with a standby generator using our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac generator cost guide</a>, and with a fuel-burning unit using our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a>. For long outages with heavy loads, gas or propane is often still the cheaper answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-home winner: EcoFlow. The Delta Pro 3 is the more complete, more proven home system. Jackery's large units are worth a look if you prefer the brand or find a strong bundle, but compare the ecosystem carefully before you commit several thousand dollars to it.
