          <p>
            EcoFlow's ecosystem is broader and older. If you want to start with a 1 kWh station and gradually build toward a solar-backed home system, EcoFlow has more pieces that fit together. Jackery's ecosystem is narrower but well integrated where it exists: the SolarSaga panels pair cleanly with every Explorer, and its large-model expansion batteries and transfer switch cover the home-backup basics. One caution applies to both: an ecosystem is a lock-in. Add-on batteries from one brand do not fit the other, and smart panels are brand-specific. Solar panels are the exception, which makes them the safest accessory to buy first.
          </p>

          <p>
            EcoFlow made very fast wall charging its signature early on, and Jackery has largely caught up in the v2 generation. At the 1 kWh size, both brands now quote roughly an hour from empty to full on a household outlet: the Jackery in a fast or "emergency" mode enabled through its app, the EcoFlow by default. The practical difference is small, but a Jackery straight out of the box may charge more slowly until you change the setting. Fast charging matters in a rolling outage, where the grid flickers back for an hour or two between failures; a station that refills in an hour is ready for the next drop, and one that takes four or five hours is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Refill Times</h3>

