          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apps</h3>

          <p>
            Both brands now offer phone apps that connect over Wi-Fi or Bluetooth and show state of charge, input and output watts, and estimated runtime. EcoFlow's app is the deeper of the two: it exposes charge and discharge limits, charging speed, AC and DC output toggles, standby timers and, on the larger units, home-panel circuit control and energy scheduling. That depth is great for people who like to tune a system and slightly overwhelming for those who do not. Jackery's app is simpler, and the stations are designed to be used without it; the main reason to install it is to switch on fast charging and adjust a few battery-protection settings. If you are buying for a parent who will never open an app, that counts in Jackery's favor.
          </p>

          <p>
            One tip for either brand: set the charge limits and update the firmware once, at home, before you need the station. Outages are a bad time to discover that a feature lives behind an app login, and a cellular network under storm load is a bad time to download a firmware update.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranty, Support and Longevity</h3>

          <p>
            Both brands offer roughly five years of warranty coverage on their current LFP models, the clearest signal that each trusts its cells and inverters for the long haul. Terms vary by model, by retailer and by whether you register the product, so read the warranty page for the specific unit and keep proof of purchase. Both companies are large and widely stocked in the U.S., with replacement cables, chargers and accessories easy to find. Both use LFP chemistry rated for thousands of cycles, so either should outlast the warranty with sensible storage: keep it at 50 to 80% charge, top it up every few months, and avoid charging below freezing. If the station lives in a damp basement next to a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a>, keep it off the floor and away from condensation.
          </p>
