        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by brand before choosing by size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The capacity and output decision matters far more than the logo. A 1 kWh EcoFlow and a 1 kWh Jackery run a fridge for nearly the same time, while either one is wrong if your load list needs 2 kWh. Add up your average watts and hours first, then pick the brand that fits that size class.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming 1,500 W runs every kitchen appliance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kettles, toasters and hair dryers often draw 1,500 to 1,800 W, which puts them right at or past the Explorer 1000 v2's continuous limit. If coffee or microwave meals are part of your outage plan, check the wattage label on each appliance before deciding a 1,500 W inverter is enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a fixed-size unit when needs may grow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery's popular v2 models cannot take an add-on battery, so outgrowing one means selling it or buying a second unit. If there is any chance you will want 2 kWh or a home panel later, starting on an expandable EcoFlow platform avoids paying twice for the same capacity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting rated solar wattage from folding panels</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200 W panel does not put 200 W into the battery all day. Expect about 60 to 75 percent of rating in good midday sun and four to five peak sun hours, so a 200 W panel realistically adds roughly 480 to 750 Wh on a clear day, less in winter or cloud.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the app setup until the outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery's fastest wall charging is switched on in its app, and EcoFlow hides charge limits and output modes there too. Install the app, update firmware and set charge limits at home while the power is on, because storms take down both the grid and the cellular data you need.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is EcoFlow or Jackery better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better across the board. EcoFlow generally offers more output at each size class, add-on batteries, smart home panels and a deeper app, which makes it stronger for home backup and growing needs. Jackery generally offers lighter units for the capacity, simpler controls and the best single-box 2 kWh option, which suits camping and RV use. Both use LFP batteries on current models and offer roughly five years of warranty coverage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a Jackery solar generator and a Jackery power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only the bundle. A Jackery solar generator is an Explorer power station sold together with one or more SolarSaga folding panels and the cables to connect them; the station inside is identical to the one sold alone. An EcoFlow solar generator works the same way. Bundles can be good value when discounted, but you can also buy the station alone and add panels later, including third-party panels within its input limits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for camping, EcoFlow or Jackery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most campers Jackery has the edge at 1 kWh and above because its units are lighter: the Explorer 1000 v2 stores 1,070 Wh at about 24 pounds against about 28 pounds for the Delta 3 Plus, and the Explorer 2000 v2 puts 2 kWh in one 40-pound box. At the small end, EcoFlow's River 3 Plus wins with a 600 W inverter and a snap-on extra battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for home backup during power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EcoFlow is the stronger home-backup platform. The Delta 3 Plus offers 1,800 W, enough for a microwave alongside a fridge, and accepts add-on batteries. The Delta Pro 3 combines 4,096 Wh, 4,000 W and native 120/240 V with a smart home panel and high solar input. Jackery's large Explorer Plus systems also expand, but the ecosystem is narrower. Hardwired circuits need an electrician-installed transfer switch or interlock.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Jackery solar panels with an EcoFlow power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, with the right adapter. A panel simply supplies DC power, so what matters is that its voltage and current, alone or wired with others, fall within the station's published solar input range and that the connectors match. Series wiring raises voltage, and cold sunny mornings raise it further, so leave a margin. Add-on batteries and smart panels, by contrast, are brand-specific and never cross over.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will an EcoFlow or Jackery power station run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use runtime equals watt-hours times 0.85, divided by the average load. A modern fridge uses about 1 to 2 kWh a day, roughly 40 to 85 watts on average. On that basis a Delta 3 Plus or Explorer 1000 v2 runs a typical fridge for about 10 to 20 hours, a 2 kWh unit for roughly a day, and the Delta Pro 3 for about two days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are EcoFlow and Jackery power stations expandable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most EcoFlow stations are. The River 3 Plus takes a snap-on extra battery, the Delta 3 Plus pairs with extra batteries to reach about 2 kWh and beyond, and the Delta Pro 3 scales into the tens of kilowatt-hours. Jackery's popular Explorer v2 models are fixed-capacity, so the upgrade path is a larger or second unit, although its large Explorer Plus home-backup models do support expansion batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

